%description:

Tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment:off -- no segment carries PSH.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "set PSH on the last buffered segment"; false sets none.

The classes: on (true, the default), off (false; LegacyTcp). The code: PSH on the segment
after which no queued data waits, unless pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary replaces the rule
(TcpConnection::sendSegment()). This test separates "off" from "on".

Host under test: host1, which sends 100 bytes. Observation: level 1, the data segment has no
PSH.

%# parameter: Tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment
%# class: off
%# value: false
%# key: *.host1.tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOff")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.pshBit == false")
             .describe("the data segment of host1, without PSH")
             .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOff compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.pushLastBufferedSegment = false    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100B
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpPushLastBufferedSegmentOff: PASS
